      "dom_id": "question-59512",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-3\\le 2x-7\\le 1$ or $2+3x<2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $-3\\le 2x-7\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\le 2x-7\\le 1 \\\\ 4 & \\le 2x\\le 8 \\\\ 2 & \\le x\\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252742/assets/909a1923438f6bf6931373c1a29cde24cd6ed08166c96769afa10a9a28696113.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2+3x<2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3x & <2 \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & <\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ x & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252742/assets/daafda364f7c22d6db174e8fcc63092b754ca124854aa24a18bb7f0df024981c.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252742/assets/b84ab4b3eeab7754193a0a41a2a1dc86338f7f0bed682b86b74fbe83cab6324d.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x<0$ or $2\\le x\\le 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup [2,4].$",
